#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151910 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "88fe37cf-5df2-5988-90b8-082f7aaca609" :question/math-academy-id "e-6791" :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem:\n\n$y'' + y = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = 2, \\quad y'(\\pi) = -1$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= -1 \\\\\nλ &= ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A \\cos x + B \\sin x\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find constants $A$ and $B$. To do this, we apply the initial conditions $y(\\pi)=2$, $y'(\\pi)=-1$.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\pi) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n$y(π)|= 2; A\\cos π + B\\sin π|= 2; - A|= 2; A|=-2$.\n- Computing $y'(x)$, we get\n$y' =-A \\sin{x} + B\\cos{x}$,\nand substituting $y'(\\pi)=-1$, we get\n$y^{′}(π)|=-1; - A\\sin π + B\\cos π|=-1; - B|=-1; B|= 1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -2\\cos{x} + \\sin{x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151921 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "4739ced1-bbcc-516b-971f-a580ec97961a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6861" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$y'' + y - 2y =0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y = Ae^{x} + Be^{-2 x}$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n$y'' + y - 2y =0, \\ \\ y(1) = e, \\ \\ y'(1) = e ^{-2}$." :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n$e|= Ae^{1} + Be^{-2(1)}; e|= Ae + Be^{-2}; A|= 1 - Be^{-3}$.\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2}$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-x}$.\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n$e^{-2}|= Ae^{1} - 2Be^{-2(1)}; e^{-2}|= Ae - 2Be^{-2}$.\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{A = 1 - Be^{-3}; e^{-2} = Ae - 2Be^{-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nA = 1 - B e ^{-3}\n$$\n\nin the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{-2} &= (1 - Be^{-3})e - 2Be^{-2} \\\\\n1 &= e^{3} - B - 2B \\\\\nB &= \\frac{e^{3} - 1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting\n\n$$\nB= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3\n$$\n\nback into the equation\n\n$$\nA = 1-Be^{-3}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\nA = 1 - \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 = \\dfrac {2 + e ^ {-3}} 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac {e ^ {-3} + 2} 3 e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151922 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "95cdd4cc-df70-5dac-8bd3-66edb012cdcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6862" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$y'' - 4 y' + 4y =0, \\qquad y(0) = 3, \\qquad y'(0) = 2$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 4λ + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 2)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 2\n$$\n\nso we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A e ^ {2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=3$ into our general solution gives\n$3|= Ae^{2(0)} + B(0)e^{2(0)}; 3|= Ae^{0} + 0; 3|= A$.\n- To apply the condition $y' (0) = 2$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y' = 2 A e ^ {2 x} + B e ^ {2 x} + 2 B x e ^{2 x}$.\nThen, we substitute $y'(0)=2$ into the above to get\n$2|= 2Ae^{2(0)} + Be^{2(0)} + 2B(0)e^{2(0)}; 2|= 2Ae^{0} + Be^{0} + 0; 2|= 2A + B$.\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{3 = A; 2 = 2A + B\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=3$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= 6 + B \\\\\nB &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3$ and $B=-4$, and therefore the solution is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e ^ {2 x} - 4 x e ^ {2 x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151935 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "80968044-25e2-5906-929c-b944f84ca27d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7051" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$y'' - 6y' + 25y=0, \\quad y(0)= 2, \\quad y'(0) = 5, \\quad t\\gt 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 6λ + 25 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 6λ + 9) - 9 + 25 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 3)^{2} + 16 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 3)^{2} &= -16 \\\\\nλ - 3 &= 4\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= 3 ± 4\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left(P \\cos 4x + Q \\sin 4x \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n$2 = P$.\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 5$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y^{′}|= 3e^{3x}(P\\cos 4x + Q\\sin 4x) + e^{3x}(-4P\\sin 4x + 4Q\\cos 4x)$\nThen, we substitute $y'(0) = 5$ and $P=2$ into the above to get\n$5|= 3e^{0}(2\\cos 0 + Q\\sin 0) + e^{0}(-4(2)\\sin 0 + 4Q\\cos 0); 5|= 6 + 4Q; - 1|= 4Q; Q|=-\\frac{1}{4}$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left(2 \\cos 4x - \\dfrac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9695b201-3ea6-5309-879a-caf031732b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3e^{-3x} - 6e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151099} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4e31dd-6ad4-543b-bcab-b26c6a17ced1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 7e^{-3x} - 10e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151100} {:answer/id #uuid "00a553cf-b153-5ec4-a802-b73f8922ab27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-10e^{-3x} + 7e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151101} {:answer/id #uuid "02886e96-bf85-5930-b7fa-116bc64c9bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-9e^{-3x} + 6e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151102} {:answer/id #uuid "73b66be1-6e46-53b0-9f89-534c0ab22f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-12e^{-3x} + 9e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00a553cf-b153-5ec4-a802-b73f8922ab27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-10e^{-3x} + 7e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151101} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c1c0b2e-1224-5711-8b65-104884615486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151098}] :question/id #uuid "de2c9bcd-a587-5660-97a8-aa4955c427cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-7788" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$y^{″} + 7y^{′} + 12y = 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y = Ae^{-3x} + Be^{-4x}$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$y^{″} + 7y^{′} + 12y = 0, y(0) =-3, y^{′}(0) = 2$."}]}
